                  "language" : null,
                  "authority" : null,
                  "confidence" : -1,
                  "place" : 0
                } ],
                "dc.description.abstract" : [ {
                  "value" : "Summary of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 \nObjectives of the Act\n•\tTo provide a comprehensive legal framework for domestic and international commercial arbitration. \n•\tTo facilitate the speedy and efficient resolution of disputes through arbitration and conciliation. \n•\tTo minimise judicial intervention in arbitral proceedings. \n•\tTo ensure the recognition and enforcement of domestic and foreign arbitral awards. \n•\tTo promote India as an arbitration-friendly jurisdiction. \nKey Provisions\n•\tArbitration Agreement: The Act recognises and enforces written arbitration agreements, under which parties agree to refer disputes to arbitration instead of litigation. \n•\tAppointment of Arbitrators: It lays down the procedure for the appointment, challenge, replacement, and termination of arbitrators, while ensuring their independence and impartiality. \n•\tConduct of Arbitral Proceedings: The Act provides flexibility to parties in determining the procedure for arbitration, subject to the principles of fairness, equality, and natural justice. \n•\tArbitral Award: Arbitral tribunals are empowered to make final and binding awards. The Act specifies the form, contents, and grounds for correction, interpretation, and challenge of awards. \n•\tCourt Assistance and Limited Judicial Intervention: Courts may assist in matters such as appointment of arbitrators, grant of interim measures, and enforcement of awards, but judicial intervention is otherwise limited to the circumstances expressly provided in the Act. \n•\tEnforcement of Awards: Domestic arbitral awards are enforceable as decrees of a court. The Act also provides for the recognition and enforcement of foreign arbitral awards under the New York Convention and the Geneva Convention, subject to specified conditions. \n•\tConciliation: The Act establishes a statutory framework for conciliation, enabling parties to resolve disputes amicably with the assistance of an impartial conciliator. A settlement agreement reached through conciliation has the same status and effect as an arbitral award on agreed terms. \n•\tRule-making Power: The Central Government is empowered to make rules for carrying out the provisions of the Act.",
                  "language" : null,
                  "authority" : null,
                  "confidence" : -1,
                  "place" : 0
                } ],
                "dc.identifier.act_id" : [ {
                  "value" : "AC_CEN_3_46_00004_199626_1517807323919",
